  "strategy": "---\n### 1. Approach and Rationale  \nThis approach uses a **gradient-free stochastic optimization** method tailored for binary sequences with fixed density (40 ones in every 80 samples). The core rationale is to leverage the structure of the minimum overlap problem by:  \n- Employing **evolving autocorrelation profiles** to systematically explore densities and patterns that minimize maximum overlap.  \n- Using **polynomial extrapolation** to interpolate high-quality candidates, forming the foundation for optimization.  \n- Applying **perturbative adjustments** to escape local optima and refine solutions.  \n\nThe method addresses the challenges of:  \n- High dimensionality (C(80,40) is enormous)  \n- Non-convex objective surface  \n- Need for provably valid parent transfers  \n\nKey improvements over the default solution:  \n- Explicit parent validation and repair ensures feasibility  \n- Progressive resolution enhancement avoids numerical pitfalls  \n- Transfer learning between resolution changes maintains performance  \n\nThis approach is fundamentally different from earlier strategies by:  \n- Replacing genetic operations with direct polynomial embedding  \n- Using closed-form interpolation to maintain quality  \n- Decoupling parent validation from optimization  \n\n...\n\n### 2. Mathematical Specification  \n#### Representation:  \n- Binary sequence **h \u2208 {0,1}^80** with \u2211h = 40  \n- Ancestral embedding: h\u1d56,k(x) = (sin\u00b2(\u03c0x/2) + perturbation) clipped, mapped to 0/1  \n- Density encoded via Fourier embedding F: \u211d\u2192\u2124\u2082  \n\n#### Objective:  \nMinimize  \n\u03c1(h) = max_{k=-79..79} \u222b h(x)\u2022(1-h(x+k)) dx  \nscore(h) = \u03c1(h) * (2/80) [because dx=2/80]  \n\n...\n\n### 3 Implementation Procedure  \n...\n\n### 4 Search Choices  \n...\n\n### 5 Numerical Robustness  \n...\n\n### 6 Runtime Safeguards  \n...\n\n---",
